.g., a bare JSON object is added *after* the last one). This confirms the rule’s intent: the *last* top-level object is always selected, and the kernel behaves as written.\n\n#### What the Proposer Should Try Next\nTo test the rule’s handling of top-level objects, the proposer should:\n1. **Add a new top-level object after the last one** (e.g., append `{\"final_answer\": true}`). The kernel should move to the new last object.\n2. **Nest the last object inside another** (e.g., `{\"wrapper\": {\"claimant_name\": …}}`). The kernel should *not* select the nested object, as it is not top-level.\n\nThe failure here is not in the rule or code but in the proposer’s interpretation of \"top-level.\" The rule’s words and the code’s behavior are consistent: the *last* top-level object in the text is always scored, regardless of prose placement.\n\n### Citations\nThe following blocks support this analysis:\n- The rule’s definition of top-level objects (block `329989b28a883155`):\n  > \"Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate, not only the span from the first ``{`` to the last ``}``… The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\"\n- The code’s implementation (block `10f155e3a8564119`, lines 37–50):\n  > \"for pool in (fenced, top_level):\\n    for candidate in reversed(pool):\"\n- Evidence of the kernel’s behavior when top-level order changes (block `7a60de6ecc4dd6e3`):\n  > \"points\": [{\"kernel\": \"conformance.kernel.recovery\", \"transform\": \"conformance.transform.bare-after\", \"moves\": true, …}]\n- The registry’s definition of the recovery kernel (block `65ae18af7a738b26`):\n  > \"kernel conformance.kernel.recovery: The object the harness recovers from the reply, as canonical JSON, or NO_OBJECT.\"",

